Elementwise characterisation of the least map of a step list as a pointwise supremum of principals.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`leastMapVals`).** At an ideal element, the value set of a step list is the set of principals of those right factors whose left factors contain the argument.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Theorem (`Element.mem_interYs_of`).** Membership of an ideal element in an intersection of range neighbourhoods is membership in the master together with membership in each active right factor.

*Proof.* Unpack the defining quantification. ∎

**Theorem (`leastMap_toElementMap_le_of_ub`).** The least map of a consistent step list is below every map that relates each listed pair.

*Proof.* Bound the value via the relating neighbourhoods of the upper bound. ∎

**Theorem (`val_le_leastMap_toElementMap`).** Each principal arising from an active right factor lies below the value of the least map.

*Proof.* The least map relates the corresponding left factor to that right factor. ∎

**Theorem (`leastMapVals_bounded`).** Under consistency of the step list, the value set at each argument is bounded.

*Proof.* The value of the least map is a common upper bound. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_leastMap_eq_sSup`).** The value of the least map at an argument is the supremum of the corresponding value set.

*Proof.* The least map is an upper bound, and every upper bound of the step list dominates that supremum. ∎
